<BaseLayout title="Relayo — technical case study" description="Relayo is a central notification intake service for external applications, with API integrations, a personal inbox, and Web Push." lang="en" alternate="/projects/relayo/">
  <article class="container case">
    <a class="back" href="/en/#projects">← Home</a>
    <header class="hero"><p class="eyebrow">ENGINEERING CASE · PERSONAL PROJECT</p><h1>Relayo</h1><p class="lead">A central inbox for events from Bitrix, Laravel, cron, monitoring, and other applications—without tying senders to a particular messenger.</p><p class="status">MVP implemented · production operation is unconfirmed</p><p class="stack">Laravel · Vue 3 · MySQL · Redis · REST API · Web Push · PWA</p></header>

    <section><h2>The problem</h2><p>Each external project has its own way to report an order, an error, or a completed task. Relayo provides a common API and user inbox so senders do not depend on Telegram or a separate interface for every source.</p><p>An integration submits an event to one of the user’s channels. Relayo checks a Bearer token, stores the notification, and displays it in the web application; when a Push subscription is enabled, it also starts an asynchronous attempt to notify the browser.</p></section>

    <section><h2>Architecture</h2><p>The Laravel backend provides a REST API and SPA authentication with Sanctum cookie sessions and CSRF. A Vue 3 SPA using Pinia and Vue Router manages channels, tokens, the inbox, and Push subscriptions. MySQL stores users, channels, tokens, notifications, and subscriptions; Redis is used for sessions and queues in the example environment configuration.</p><p>NotificationService connects the API contract to the notification model and the background job. SendWebPushNotification loads the stored notification and passes it to WebPushService. At the current scope this is a connected Laravel monolith: separate services would add network boundaries and operational work without a demonstrated need.</p></section>

    <section><h2>Channels and notification model</h2><p>A channel belongs to a user and names an integration category such as `orders` or `monitoring`. An external request contains the channel slug, title, message, level (`info`, `success`, `warning`, `error`), and optional URL and JSON payload.</p><p>A notification is associated with its user and channel and stores content, level, and read state. The inbox supports pagination, filters by channel/level/read state, search, marking one or all as read, and deletion. An inbox record exists independently from whether Push succeeds.</p></section>

    <section><h2>API tokens and data isolation</h2><p>A token is created for a user, shown in plaintext only at creation, and stored as a SHA-256 hash. An expiration can be set; middleware hashes the Bearer token and rejects unknown or expired values. A successful submission updates `last_used_at`.</p><p>NotificationService looks up a channel through the token owner’s relationship, so a token cannot send into another user’s channel. The inbox is queried through the current user relationship; read and delete operations likewise find notifications only among that user’s records. The SPA uses a separate Sanctum session and CSRF protection.</p></section>

    <section><h2>Receiving and viewing notifications</h2><p>The dashboard loads channels and notifications and supports filtering, search, marking as read, and deletion. While the page remains open, the inbox refreshes through polling every 15 seconds. This is simpler for an MVP and avoids persistent realtime connections, but new data can wait until the next poll and the API receives periodic requests.</p></section>

    <section><h2>Web Push, PWA, and background jobs</h2><p>Web Push is implemented: a user can request browser permission, fetch the public VAPID key, and save a subscription on the server. The Service Worker registers in production builds, handles push events, and opens the notification link on click. A manifest describes the installable application; the Service Worker caches the shell and static GET resources while excluding API and Sanctum requests.</p><p>If VAPID keys are not configured, WebPushService returns without sending Push. Notifications still remain in the database and are available through inbox polling. Endpoint failures are logged; expired subscriptions are deleted. There is no built-in email fallback, delivery status on a notification, or confirmation that the device displayed it.</p></section>

    <section class="deep"><p class="eyebrow">DEEPER ENGINEERING DIVE</p><h2>Why notification intake and delivery should be separate</h2><p>An external system sent a request and received a response. That does not mean the user saw a Push notification. There are distinct boundaries between API authentication, storing the record, dispatching a job, processing the queue, the Push provider response, and the browser displaying a notification.</p>
      <figure class="flow"><figcaption>From incoming event to attempted notification display</figcaption><div class="flow-scroll"><svg viewBox="0 0 1400 590" role="img" aria-labelledby="relayo-flow-title relayo-flow-desc" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><title id="relayo-flow-title">Accepting an event and attempting browser Push</title><desc id="relayo-flow-desc">An external system calls the API. Middleware authenticates the Bearer token hash. NotificationService verifies the token owner's channel, writes the notification and last_used_at to MySQL, then dispatches SendWebPushNotification with afterCommit. The API returns 201. A queue worker loads the notification and calls WebPushService. WebPushService sends to the Push endpoint; the Browser Service Worker receives the push event and calls showNotification. A 201 response and a successful endpoint response do not confirm that the user saw the notification.</desc><defs><marker id="relayo-arrow" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" refX="7" refY="4" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0L8 4L0 8" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/></marker></defs><g class="actor"><rect x="15" y="24" width="150" height="58" rx="8"/><text x="90" y="48" class="ttl" text-anchor="middle">External system</text><text x="90" y="68" class="sub" text-anchor="middle">Bitrix / cron</text></g><g class="actor"><rect x="210" y="24" width="155" height="58" rx="8"/><text x="287" y="48" class="ttl" text-anchor="middle">API Token</text><text x="287" y="68" class="sub" text-anchor="middle">middleware</text></g><g class="actor"><rect x="410" y="24" width="185" height="58" rx="8"/><text x="502" y="48" class="ttl" text-anchor="middle">NotificationService</text><text x="502" y="68" class="sub" text-anchor="middle">validate + create</text></g><g class="actor"><rect x="640" y="24" width="145" height="58" rx="8"/><text x="712" y="48" class="ttl" text-anchor="middle">MySQL</text><text x="712" y="68" class="sub" text-anchor="middle">inbox + token</text></g><g class="actor"><rect x="830" y="24" width="155" height="58" rx="8"/><text x="907" y="48" class="ttl" text-anchor="middle">Queue Job</text><text x="907" y="68" class="sub" text-anchor="middle">worker</text></g><g class="actor"><rect x="1030" y="24" width="165" height="58" rx="8"/><text x="1112" y="48" class="ttl" text-anchor="middle">WebPushService</text><text x="1112" y="68" class="sub" text-anchor="middle">Push endpoint</text></g><g class="actor"><rect x="1240" y="24" width="145" height="58" rx="8"/><text x="1312" y="48" class="ttl" text-anchor="middle">Browser</text><text x="1312" y="68" class="sub" text-anchor="middle">Service Worker</text></g><path class="life" d="M90 82V545M287 82V545M502 82V545M712 82V545M907 82V545M1112 82V545M1312 82V545"/><path class="msg" d="M165 125H202" marker-end="url(#relayo-arrow)"/><text x="183" y="115" class="lbl" text-anchor="middle">1 · POST</text><path class="msg" d="M365 165H402" marker-end="url(#relayo-arrow)"/><text x="383" y="155" class="lbl" text-anchor="middle">2 · user + token</text><path class="msg" d="M595 205H632" marker-end="url(#relayo-arrow)"/><text x="613" y="195" class="lbl" text-anchor="middle">3 · save row</text><path class="msg" d="M632 240H595" marker-end="url(#relayo-arrow)"/><text x="613" y="230" class="lbl" text-anchor="middle">4 · persisted</text><path class="msg" d="M595 275H822" marker-end="url(#relayo-arrow)"/><text x="708" y="265" class="lbl" text-anchor="middle">5 · dispatch afterCommit</text><path class="msg" d="M410 315H165" marker-end="url(#relayo-arrow)"/><text x="287" y="305" class="lbl" text-anchor="middle">6 · HTTP 201 Created</text><path class="msg" d="M907 355H632" marker-end="url(#relayo-arrow)"/><text x="770" y="345" class="lbl" text-anchor="middle">7 · load notification + user</text><path class="msg" d="M632 390H907" marker-end="url(#relayo-arrow)"/><text x="770" y="380" class="lbl" text-anchor="middle">8 · record + subscriptions</text><path class="msg" d="M985 430H1022" marker-end="url(#relayo-arrow)"/><text x="1003" y="420" class="lbl" text-anchor="middle">9 · send job</text><path class="msg" d="M1195 475H1232" marker-end="url(#relayo-arrow)"/><text x="1213" y="465" class="lbl" text-anchor="middle">10 · push event</text><text x="24" y="565" class="sub">201 confirms API handling and the inbox record, not that the browser displayed a Push notification.</text></svg></div><p class="flow-note">`afterCommit()` defers dispatch until an enclosing database transaction commits. NotificationService does not open a transaction itself; without an outer transaction, dispatch is issued after its writes in the normal request flow.</p></figure>
      <h3>What each stage actually confirms</h3><ul><li><strong>API authentication:</strong> an active token was found; this authorizes the integration.</li><li><strong>Inbox record:</strong> Notification was saved in MySQL and the API builds a `201 Created` response.</li><li><strong>Queue dispatch:</strong> the code called dispatch with `afterCommit`; processing depends on the queue backend and a running worker.</li><li><strong>Push attempt:</strong> the worker called WebPushService and received endpoint reports from the Push library.</li><li><strong>Browser display:</strong> the Service Worker received a push event and called `showNotification`; the server gets no confirmation that a person saw it.</li></ul><p>When an endpoint fails, WebPushService logs a warning; expired subscriptions are removed. The Job does not define explicit `tries` or `backoff`, and per-subscription errors are caught inside the service. This is a best-effort attempt, not a delivery guarantee. If VAPID is not configured, sending is skipped, while the stored inbox item remains available.</p>
    </section>

    <section><h2>Testing and limitations</h2><p>Feature tests cover a successful external POST, record creation and queue dispatch, rejection of an invalid API token, inbox filtering, marking as read, deletion, token management, and Push subscription management. Tests also verify that a user cannot manage another user’s notifications or tokens.</p><p>The reviewed Feature tests assert job dispatch but do not exercise end-to-end delivery through a real Push provider or display on a device. There is no explicit retry/backoff policy or delivery status in the Job. Docker Compose includes Redis but does not define a queue worker process; a worker must be started separately to process Redis jobs. Polling remains the primary inbox refresh mechanism.</p></section>

    <section><h2>Implemented and next</h2><div class="status-grid"><article><h3>Implemented</h3><ul><li>Bearer API tokens stored as hashes, with expiry and revocation.</li><li>Channels and external notification intake.</li><li>Inbox with search, filters, 15-second polling, and read-state controls.</li><li>Asynchronous Push attempts through a queue and VAPID subscriptions.</li><li>Service Worker, Web Push, manifest, and a basic PWA/offline shell.</li></ul></article><article><h3>Limitations and possible next steps</h3><ul><li>No confirmed delivery guarantee or end-to-end delivery status.</li><li>No explicit Push job retry/backoff or email fallback.</li><li>Polling could be replaced with event-driven delivery if needed.</li><li>Teams/shared channels, integration SDKs, and richer observability need separate development.</li></ul></article></div><p class="note">The repository is private. Source code and configuration are not published; I’m happy to discuss architecture and selected implementation details in a technical interview.</p></section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
